@@ -429,3 +429,145 @@ rustfmt gate caught one formatting miss — cargo fmt before re-run.
 **Next**: full bzImage rebuild with the fixed compiler (running),
 boot validation of defect (d) (expect no bad-pte BUG), then defect
 (b) CPU1 SIPI, then the CachyOS 6.18 LTS performance comparison.
+
+## 09-18 (session 2) — defect (g) FIXED: inline-asm tied operands with no-slot inputs; kernel boots to userspace; NEW defect (h) exposed
+
+**Symptom (pre-fix)**: kernel booted to `Run /init as init process`
+and userspace stalled forever. IR pre-codegen was verified CORRECT
+(`Cmp Eq V27,V56`); the miscompile was pure backend: in
+`__get_file_rcu` (fs/file.c, 6.18.52) the `OPTIMIZER_HIDE_VAR`
+asm (`"" : "=r"(out) : "0"(in)`) compared a leftover `=@ccns`
+boolean chain in %rcx against the file pointer in %r12 — the tie
+load of `in` was never emitted.
+
+**Root cause**: `load_input_to_reg` (x86-64 asm_emitter) handled
+`Operand::Value` inputs by loading from their STACK SLOT only. When
+the tied input value is register-homed WITHOUT a slot (value
+forwarding kept the fresh `Load *f` in %r13), the load was silently
+skipped — the asm operand register kept whatever it held. Same
+latent hole in i686 and ARM (RISC-V had the home path but silently
+skipped home-less+slot-less values).
+
+**Fix (5 files)**:
+- x86-64: no-slot inputs materialise via `value_to_reg` (fresh/stale
+  homes, width-consistent typed loads, Copy/bit-identical Cast
+  chains, GlobalAddr remats; panics loudly otherwise).
+- `value_to_reg_inner`: added the accumulator (rax) last-resort
+  fallback before the final panic, mirroring `operand_to_callee_reg`.
+- i686: no-slot inputs load from their authoritative linear-scan home
+  (`movl %home, %reg`); 64-bit pairs and home-less+slot-less values
+  panic loudly.
+- ARM: no-slot inputs `mov` from `reg_assignments` home (RISC-V-style);
+  FP/no-home panic loudly.
+- RISC-V: home-less+slot-less inputs panic loudly (was silent skip).
+
+**Validation**:
+- Kernel repro (exact fs/file.o .cmd replay): the tie copy
+  `mov %r13,%rcx` now emitted; `cmp %rcx,%r12` (bool-vs-pointer)
+  became `cmp %r13,%r12` (pointer-vs-pointer).
+- New unit tests `tied_operand_no_slot_tests` (4): tie copy from
+  home, from accumulator, no-op when home==scratch, width preserved —
+  all fail by construction pre-fix.
+- New regression `inline_asm_tied_operand_hide_var.c`
+  (userspace `__get_file_rcu` lookalike: hide-var tie + `lock addq`
+  + `=@ccns` overflow + reload-compare) passes at -O0/-O1/-O2 and
+  agrees with GCC; note: it does NOT trigger the no-slot path on its
+  own (same lesson as repro.c–repro7.c — only the exact kernel flags/
+  pressure shape does), so it guards the semantics, while the unit
+  tests pin the path.
+- cargo test 2869/0; asmdiff CI gate (i686) 20/20; 64-bit corpus
+  276 pre-existing 2.44-canonical diffs (journal 09-17 session 1
+  measured 283 — no new failures from this change); ci_local --fast
+  43/0/3 ALL GATES GREEN; rustfmt clean.
+- Full VM-config bzImage rebuild (allnoconfig + kernel-vm.fragment,
+  CC=lccc LD=lccc-ld): PASS, sha256
+  db9a4446097bae4b4a4ee414d59fd325ccfc6e2a48eb95e9b6d2efe3d4320438,
+  setup 29856/32768.
+- **QEMU boot**: `Run /init as init process` → init executes the
+  full busybox validation suite → clean ACPI S5 poweroff. Defect (g)
+  is boot-validated. (No lccc-built kernel had ever run userspace
+  before: M3 ended at run_init_process, (g) stalled init.)
+
+**NEW defect (h) exposed by userspace now running**: the init
+suite's stdout on ttyS0 arrives GARBLED — a `\r` before almost every
+character and characters out of order (e.g. `=\r-\rL\r-\rC\rC…`),
+while kernel printk lines are clean. Kernel console writes bypass
+the tty layer, userspace writes go through n_tty → 8250 TX, so the
+defect is in the lccc-built kernel's userspace serial TX path
+(suspects: n_tty echo/ONLCR processing inserting \r per char;
+possible write-chunk reordering). The boot script's verdict greps
+fail only because of this mangling — the suite itself ran to
+completion (poweroff is its last statement).
+
+**Also this session**: upstream main moved to ac65e0ad (PR #552 =
+the S07 assembler fixes merged upstream); rebase of `work` onto it
+is pending (S07 commit should drop as already-applied), `.base_ref`
+to re-anchor. asmdiff oracle (binutils 2.47 from source) still
+pending.
+
+**Next**: root-cause defect (h) (n_tty/serial TX), then (b) CPU1
+SIPI, modules (aes-ctr .ko), then CachyOS perf comparison.
+
+## Session S09 — defect (h) fixed and boot-validated (2026-09-18)
+
+**Root cause of (h), finally pinned**: not the parser, not
+type_builder, not sema. The lowerer's `sizeof_deref`
+(src/ir/lowering/expr_sizeof.rs) clamped every pointee resolving to
+size 0 up to 1 (`if sz == 0 { return 1 }`), and `sizeof_subscript`
+clamped every element size with `.max(1)` — stale guards from the
+era before the GNU `sizeof(*void_ptr) == 1` / `sizeof(*func_ptr) ==
+1` extensions got their own explicit match arms just above. GNU C
+zero-length arrays are legitimately size 0 and GCC preserves that
+through every spelling: direct decl `char (*p)[0]`, typedef
+`typedef char T0[0]; T0 *q`, struct field (the kfifo `rectype`
+idiom), subscript, nested `[0][N]`, cast, typeof. The clamp made
+`__kfifo_max_r` compute recsize 1 for every plain kfifo, so
+kfifo_in/out took the record path and the 8250 xmit kfifo delivered
+userspace serial one byte per record — the "garbled ttyS0" from the
+S08 session was a kfifo record-mode corruption, not an n_tty
+ONLCR/echo bug (that was the S08 suspicion, wrong branch).
+
+**Fix**: remove the clamp in `sizeof_deref`'s Pointer and Array arms
+and all nine `.max(1)` in `sizeof_subscript` (both subscript
+directions + the member-access fallback). The genuine 1s keep their
+explicit arms. Pointer-arithmetic strides and compound-literal
+element sizing stay clamped by design (a 0 stride in generated
+scaling code would loop; nothing in the kernel needs a 0 stride).
+Audited clean alongside: `sizeof_type`, `sizeof_identifier`,
+`sizeof_member_access`, sema `sizeof_expr`/`size_ctx`,
+`resolve_ctype_size` — none clamp.
+
+**Verification chain**:
+- `sizeof_zero_length_array_pointee.c` (new regression, 31 checks
+  incl. kfifo recsize 0/1/2 and the void*/func* extensions):
+  GCC-oracle differential PASS, A/B slot differential PASS.
+- Full regression suite: **730 pass, 0 fail** (the 3 i686 build
+  failures were the wiped gcc-multilib; installed libc6-dev-i386,
+  then 13/13 i686 PASS — environmental, fixed per standing order).
+- ci_local.sh --fast: ALL GATES GREEN (43 pass).
+- Fresh full kernel rebuild (prepare_kernel_tree re-run from
+  tarball after another env wipe truncated the tree — canary
+  `scripts/Kbuild.include` missing triggered regeneration; 28
+  patches + config + build, 1081 s, setup 29856/32768).
+- QEMU boot: **16/17 PASS** — the only failure is the known (b)
+  CPU1 SIPI (identical to S08). Serial output is letter-perfect for
+  the first time: 586 clean lines, long lines intact.
+
+**Regression-proofing**: qemu_boot_test.sh now prints two
+serial-integrity sentinel lines (punctuation + hex address) and
+greps both verbatim — the old 14 checks could not have caught (h),
+because a console that corrupts the byte stream still boots and
+passes every config/dmesg grep. Defect-class coverage now permanent.
+
+**Deliverables**: commits d2243eac6 (fix + tests) and 738f4755a
+(boot sentinels) on `work` (base ac65e0ad, upstream main unchanged);
+ms178-1.patch regenerated (11 files, +637/−15, 37 483 bytes,
+applies clean on ac65e0ad); S09 snapshot taken and this time the
+bundle refresh was verified by cloning it back (HEAD 738f4755 —
+S08's silent-stale-bundle failure mode did not recur).
+
+**Next**: (b) CPU1 SIPI alive-timeout (now the sole boot-test
+failure), then modules (aes-ctr .ko end-to-end), then CachyOS 6.18
+perf comparison (re-check for a 6.18.5x point release first),
+binutils 2.47 asmdiff oracle, ICX/ICC/Clang 23.1 oracle breadth,
+7× movzbl missed-opt hunt.
diff --git a/scripts/kernel_isolate.sh b/scripts/kernel_isolate.sh
new file mode 100755
index 000000000..0fdb2738b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/scripts/kernel_isolate.sh
@@ -0,0 +1,130 @@
+#!/usr/bin/env bash
+# ============================================================================
+# kernel_isolate.sh — boot-failure isolation matrix for an LCCC-built kernel.
+#
+# When a kernel boots wrong (hang, oops, bad-pte, CPU bring-up timeout),
+# the first question is WHICH dimension is guilty: SMP? userspace init?
+# initramfs? memory size? This script runs the SAME bzImage through a
+# matrix of QEMU configurations, captures each serial log, and prints a
+# marker table so the failing dimension jumps out.
+#
+# Cases (override with KERNEL_ISOLATE_CASES="case1 case2 ..."):
+#   smp2       -smp 2, full initramfs        (the reference configuration)
+#   smp1       -smp 1, full initramfs        (SMP-sensitive defects vanish?)
+#   maxcpus1   -smp 2 + maxcpus=1            (AP present but not brought up:
+#                                             isolates SIPI/hotplug path from
+#                                             general SMP code)
+#   initsh     -smp 2, init=/bin/sh          (userspace init vs kernel)
+#   noinitrd   -smp 2, no initrd             (how far without any rootfs)
+#
+# Every case gets its own serial log + QEMU stderr under the out-dir, plus a
+# grep table of the interesting markers. Exit status is 0 unless a case fails
+# to even START (QEMU misconfiguration) — the point is diagnosis, not pass/fail.
+#
+# Usage:
+#   kernel_isolate.sh [bzImage]     (default: $KERNEL_DIR/arch/x86/boot/bzImage)
+# Environment:
+#   KERNEL_DIR     kernel tree (default /home/user/kernel-work/linux-6.18.52)
+#   QEMU           qemu binary (default qemu-system-x86_64)
+#   QEMU_DATA_DIR  firmware union dir; autodetected from the qemu install
+#   BUSYBOX        static busybox for the initramfs (default /usr/bin/busybox)
+#   OUT_DIR        report dir (default /tmp/kernel-isolate/<timestamp>)
+#   KERNEL_ISOLATE_CASES   subset of the case list above
+#   PER_CASE_TIMEOUT secs per case (default 240)
+# ============================================================================
+set -euo pipefail
+
+K=${KERNEL_DIR:-/home/user/kernel-work/linux-6.18.52}
+BZIMAGE=${1:-$K/arch/x86/boot/bzImage}
+QEMU=${QEMU:-qemu-system-x86_64}
+BUSYBOX=${BUSYBOX:-/usr/bin/busybox}
+OUT_DIR=${OUT_DIR:-/tmp/kernel-isolate/$(date +%Y%m%dT%H%M%S)}
+CASES=${KERNEL_ISOLATE_CASES:-"smp2 smp1 maxcpus1 initsh noinitrd"}
+TMO=${PER_CASE_TIMEOUT:-240}
+
+[[ -f "$BZIMAGE" ]] || { echo "kernel_isolate: bzImage missing: $BZIMAGE" >&2; exit 1; }
+command -v "$QEMU" >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo "kernel_isolate: qemu missing: $QEMU" >&2; exit 1; }
+[[ -x "$BUSYBOX" ]] || { echo "kernel_isolate: busybox missing: $BUSYBOX" >&2; exit 1; }
+
+mkdir -p "$OUT_DIR"
+
+# ---- firmware dir (BIOS + option ROM union, same rules as qemu_boot_test) ----
+qemu_l=()
+if [[ -n ${QEMU_DATA_DIR:-} ]]; then
+    [[ -f "$QEMU_DATA_DIR/bios-256k.bin" ]] || {
+        echo "kernel_isolate: QEMU_DATA_DIR has no bios-256k.bin: $QEMU_DATA_DIR" >&2; exit 1; }
+    qemu_l=(-L "$QEMU_DATA_DIR")
+else
+    qdir=$(dirname "$(command -v "$QEMU")")
+    for d in "$qdir/../share/qemu" "$qdir/../share/seabios"; do
+        if [[ -f "$d/bios-256k.bin" && -f "$d/linuxboot_dma.bin" \
+              && -f "$d/kvmvapic.bin" && -f "$d/efi-e1000.rom" ]]; then
+            qemu_l=(-L "$d"); break
+        fi
+    done
+    ((${#qemu_l[@]} > 0)) || { echo "kernel_isolate: no firmware union dir (see qemu_boot_test.sh)" >&2; exit 1; }
+fi
+
+# ---- minimal busybox initramfs -----------------------------------------------
+# Smaller than qemu_boot_test's suite: boot markers + clean poweroff only.
+WORK=$(mktemp -d)
+trap 'rm -rf "$WORK"' EXIT
+mkdir -p "$WORK/root/bin" "$WORK/root/proc" "$WORK/root/dev"
+cp "$BUSYBOX" "$WORK/root/bin/busybox"
+cat > "$WORK/root/init" <<'EOF'
+#!/bin/busybox sh
+/bin/busybox --install -s /bin
+export PATH=/bin
+echo "ISOLATE: init started"
+/bin/busybox mount -t proc proc /proc 2>/dev/null
+echo "ISOLATE: CPUs online: $(/bin/busybox cat /sys/devices/system/cpu/online 2>/dev/null || echo '?')"
+echo "ISOLATE: poweroff"
+/bin/busybox poweroff -f
+EOF
+chmod +x "$WORK/root/init"
+( cd "$WORK/root" && find . | cpio -o -H newc --quiet ) | gzip -9 > "$WORK/initramfs.cpio.gz"
+
+run_case() {
+    local name=$1 smp=$2 append_extra=$3 initrd_flag=$4
+    local log="$OUT_DIR/$name.log"
+    local cmdline="console=ttyS0,115200 nokaslr panic=-1 vga=normal $append_extra"
+    echo "== $name: -smp $smp append='$append_extra' initrd=$initrd_flag"
+    timeout "$TMO" "$QEMU" -m 512 -smp "$smp" \
+        "${qemu_l[@]}" \
+        -kernel "$BZIMAGE" \
+        $initrd_flag \
+        -display none -serial file:"$log" \
+        -no-reboot -accel tcg,thread=multi \
+        -append "$cmdline" 2> "$log.qemu-err" || true
+    # markers: last boot stage reached + defects of interest
+    local markers=("Booting the kernel" "smpboot: CPU" "bad pte" "BUG:" "panic"
+                   "Run /init" "ISOLATE: init started" "ISOLATE: CPUs online"
+                   "ISOLATE: poweroff" "reboot: Power down")
+    printf '   %-28s' "$name:"
+    for m in "${markers[@]}"; do
+        if grep -q "$m" "$log" 2>/dev/null; then
+            case $m in
+                "smpboot: CPU") printf ' cpu=%s' "$(grep -c 'smpboot: CPU' "$log")" ;;
+                *) printf ' [%s]' "$m" ;;
+            esac
+        fi
+    done
+    printf ' lines=%s\n' "$(wc -l < "$log" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)"
+}
+
+echo "kernel_isolate: $BZIMAGE"
+echo "logs + report: $OUT_DIR"
+for c in $CASES; do
+    case $c in
+        smp2)     run_case smp2     2 ""                    "-initrd $WORK/initramfs.cpio.gz" ;;
+        smp1)     run_case smp1     1 ""                    "-initrd $WORK/initramfs.cpio.gz" ;;
+        maxcpus1) run_case maxcpus1 2 "maxcpus=1"           "-initrd $WORK/initramfs.cpio.gz" ;;
+        initsh)   run_case initsh   2 "init=/bin/sh rw"     "-initrd $WORK/initramfs.cpio.gz" ;;
+        noinitrd) run_case noinitrd 2 ""                    "" ;;
+        *) echo "kernel_isolate: unknown case '$c' (skipped)" >&2 ;;
+    esac
+done
+
+echo
+echo "Full serial logs: $OUT_DIR/<case>.log — diff smp1 vs smp2 / maxcpus1 vs smp2"
+echo "to localize SMP-sensitive divergence; grep 'bad pte\|BUG\|panic' for defects."
